WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.005
The view in front of you is Earth, with everything humans have placed in its orbital neighborhood.

00:00:07.005 --> 00:00:09.150
Each dot is a real spacecraft.

00:00:09.150 --> 00:00:14.011
Each label has a builder, a launch date, an operator, and a purpose.

00:00:14.011 --> 00:00:16.441
Look at the bottom of the diagram.

00:00:16.441 --> 00:00:25.663
Down close to the surface — about four hundred kilometers up — is the International Space Station, and slightly higher, Tiangong.

00:00:25.663 --> 00:00:28.379
Both stations are continuously crewed.

00:00:28.379 --> 00:00:32.597
Right now, as you listen, there are people living up there.

00:00:32.597 --> 00:00:34.098
Look slightly higher.

00:00:34.098 --> 00:00:37.172
Five hundred fifty kilometers up is Hubble.

00:00:37.172 --> 00:00:45.965
Hubble has been watching the universe since 1990, was serviced five times by Shuttle crews, and is still returning science.

00:00:45.965 --> 00:00:54.328
Higher still — around twenty thousand kilometers — are the navigation constellations : GPS, Galileo, GLONASS, BeiDou.

00:00:54.328 --> 00:00:58.117
Without these, the maps on your phone would not work.

00:00:58.117 --> 00:01:03.264
Higher still — thirty-six thousand kilometers — is geostationary orbit .

00:01:03.264 --> 00:01:08.697
At that altitude, a satellite's orbital period is exactly twenty-four hours.

00:01:08.697 --> 00:01:16.131
Match the rotation of the Earth and the satellite appears to hang in one spot above the surface forever.

00:01:16.131 --> 00:01:21.921
Every weather satellite, every direct-broadcast television satellite, lives here.

00:01:21.921 --> 00:01:36.361
Higher still — much higher — at about one and a half million kilometers from Earth — is the second Sun-Earth Lagrange point , the place where the gravity of the Sun and the gravity of the Earth balance.

00:01:36.361 --> 00:01:39.507
The James Webb Space Telescope orbits there.

00:01:39.507 --> 00:01:44.868
So does the European Space Agency's Gaia, which is mapping a billion stars.

00:01:44.868 --> 00:01:47.584
The scale on this map is logarithmic .

00:01:47.584 --> 00:01:48.514
It has to be.

00:01:48.514 --> 00:02:01.667
Hubble at five hundred kilometers and JWST at one million five hundred thousand kilometers would, on a linear scale, be separated by a factor of three thousand seven hundred and fifty.

00:02:01.667 --> 00:02:03.526
Hubble would be invisible.

00:02:03.526 --> 00:02:11.889
The log scale gives every regime — low Earth orbit, medium Earth orbit, geostationary, beyond — its own visible band.

00:02:11.889 --> 00:02:13.390
Click any spacecraft.

00:02:13.390 --> 00:02:18.323
The panel opens with its launch story, its purpose, and its operator.

00:02:18.323 --> 00:02:31.905
Click ISS or Tiangong, and the panel offers a link to the dedicated station explorer — where every module of the station is pickable, rotatable, and attributable to the agency that built it.

00:02:31.905 --> 00:02:35.479
Earth's orbit has gotten crowded over fifty years.

00:02:35.479 --> 00:02:40.054
Most of what's up there does work you depend on without knowing.

00:02:40.054 --> 00:02:41.412
Look at the labels.

00:02:41.412 --> 00:02:42.485
Read the dates.

00:02:42.485 --> 00:02:45.201
See how recently most of this arrived.

